APOD Basic JSON
Use this when another site or app needs structured APOD data. JSON is usually the best choice when a developer or import tool wants separate fields for title, date, explanation, media type, image URL, and generated HTML.
List endpoint:
https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic
​
Paginated list:
https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic?page=1&per_page=5
​
Date range:
https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic?date_from=140901&date_to=140930
​
Single APOD by legacy date:
https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/140918
​
Supported filters:
Parameter
What it does
page
Page number for list results.
per_page
Number of results per page. Capped at 25.
date_from
Start date using legacy YYMMDD format.
date_to
End date using legacy YYMMDD format.
/apod-basic/{YYMMDD}
Gets one APOD by legacy date code.
Common JSON meta fields:
Field
Meaning
date
APOD date in YYYY-MM-DD format.
post_id
WordPress post ID.
title
APOD post title.
permalink
URL of the APOD post on the site.
media_type
Normalized APOD media type, such as image, video, or iframe.
explanation
APOD explanation text.
url
APOD post URL. This matches the permalink.
hdurl
Full-size featured image URL when available.
basic_html
APOD Basic HTML document as a JSON string.
basic_html_url
Plain-text HTML source URL for easier copy/paste workflows.
Important: JSON has to escape some characters. If you see values like \u003C, that means <. If you see \n, that means a line break. This is normal JSON behavior, not broken HTML.
For copy-and-paste HTML, use the raw HTML route instead of copying the basic_html JSON field.
APOD Basic Raw HTML (Primary for copy/paste)
Primary recommendation when a person needs to copy/paste APOD Basic HTML. This route returns the HTML document directly as plain text (not JSON), so it avoids JSON string escaping issues.
https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/140918/html
​
Use this instead of copying the basic_html JSON field whenever the goal is manual copy/paste.
APOD Basic RSS
Use this when a mirror site, feed reader, or import tool works better with RSS/XML. RSS is also useful when a system wants to check for new APOD items on a schedule.
Main feed:
https://science.nasa.gov/feed/apod-basic/
​
All APOD posts in a year:
https://science.nasa.gov/feed/apod-basic/?year=2014
​
All APOD posts in a month:
https://science.nasa.gov/feed/apod-basic/?year=2014&month=9
​
Supported RSS filters:
Parameter
What it does
No params
Most recent APOD feed items. Currently 30.
year=YYYY
All APOD posts in that year.
year=YYYY&month=M
All APOD posts in that month.
month is ignored unless year is also present.
Important RSS fields:
Field
Meaning
title
APOD title.
link
APOD post URL.
guid
Permanent item URL.
pubDate
WordPress publication date.
apod:explanation
APOD explanation text.
apod:url
APOD post URL.
apod:hdurl
Full-size featured image URL when available.
description
RSS-standard description field. It uses the same value as apod:explanation.
content:encoded
Full APOD Basic HTML document.
description is included because RSS readers and import tools commonly expect it. apod:explanation is included because APOD users are used to the old API field name. They intentionally contain the same explanation text.
The full APOD Basic HTML is inside content:encoded as CDATA. CDATA lets the RSS feed carry a full HTML document without breaking the XML feed.
Future APODs JSON
Use this for APOD posts that are scheduled for a future publish date.
https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/future-apods/
​
This endpoint is still changing, but it now includes next_apod and previous_apod data with title and URL values. Future APOD Basic HTML does not use published previous/next navigation the same way a published APOD does, because those posts have not gone live yet.
Existing Image Article RSS Feed
This is the broader WordPress feed example. It can be useful for comparison, but it is not the simplified APOD Basic feed.
https://science.nasa.gov/feed/apod-rss/
​
Use APOD Basic JSON or APOD Basic RSS when the mirror site needs the cleaner APOD plugin output.
Which Feed Should A Mirror Site Use?
For most mirror-site workflows, start with APOD Basic JSON. It provides the clearest field names and is easiest for a developer to map into another system.
Use APOD Basic RSS when the receiving system already expects RSS, XML, description, or content:encoded.
Use the raw HTML route when a person needs to copy one APOD Basic HTML document by hand.
Use Future APODs JSON only for scheduled upcoming APOD content.
